---
import Layout from '../layouts/Layout.astro';

const EMAIL = 'hello@hamzatekin.dev';
// Update both when anything below changes.
const updated = { iso: '2026-10-10', label: '10 October 2026' };
---

<Layout
  title="Privacy notice - Hamza Tekin"
  description="What personal data hamzatekin.dev handles and why. No cookies, analytics you can switch off, and your rights under UK GDPR."
>
  <article class="prose-page mb-24">
    <h1 class="text-3xl font-semibold tracking-tight text-foreground">Privacy notice</h1>
    <p class="text-sm">Last updated <time datetime={updated.iso}>{updated.label}</time></p>

    <p>
      This notice explains what personal data this website handles, why, and what you can do about it. It is written for the UK GDPR, the
      Data Protection Act 2018 and the Privacy and Electronic Communications Regulations (PECR).
    </p>

    <section aria-labelledby="summary" class="rounded-lg border border-border bg-card p-5">
      <h2 id="summary" class="pt-0!">In short</h2>
      <ul class="mt-3">
        <li>The site sets no cookies and has no advertising or cross-site tracking.</li>
        <li>
          I count page views with Cloudflare Web Analytics, which has no visitor ID and does not store your IP address. You can switch it
          off.
        </li>
        <li>If you email me, I use what you send to reply to you.</li>
      </ul>
    </section>

    <h2 id="who">Who is responsible for your data</h2>
    <p>
      I am Hamza Tekin, a software engineer based in Nottingham, UK. Under data protection law I am the controller of the personal data
      described here. You can contact me at <a href={`mailto:${EMAIL}`}>{EMAIL}</a>.
    </p>

    <h2 id="visiting">Visiting the site</h2>
    <p>
      When your browser asks for a page, it sends some information automatically, including your IP address, the address of the page and
      your browser's user agent. Cloudflare hosts the site and uses this information to deliver pages and protect the site from attacks.
      Cloudflare may also ask your browser to report failed connections to it (Network Error Logging), which helps it find network faults.
    </p>
    <p>
      I do not see these request logs. The Cloudflare dashboard shows me totals only, such as requests per day and the countries they came
      from. Cloudflare keeps its logs as described in the <a href="https://www.cloudflare.com/privacypolicy/">Cloudflare privacy policy</a>.
    </p>
    <p><strong>Lawful basis:</strong> legitimate interests, in running a website that works and stays secure.</p>

    <h2 id="analytics">Analytics</h2>
    <p>
      I use <a href="https://www.cloudflare.com/web-analytics/">Cloudflare Web Analytics</a> to see which pages people read and how they found
      the site, so I can tell what is useful and fix what is not. Cloudflare already hosts the site, so no other company is involved. I do not
      use the data for advertising or combine it with anything else.
    </p>
    <p>For each page you view, a small script from Cloudflare sends:</p>
    <ul>
      <li>the address of the page, and of the site that linked you here if there was one</li>
      <li>how long the page took to load and display</li>
      <li>a random ID for that one page view, created in your browser and not saved</li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      Cloudflare works out your country, browser, operating system and device type from the request. It discards your IP address at the
      nearest data centre and does not store it. There is no visitor ID: the script uses no cookies or local storage and does not
      fingerprint your device, so nothing links one page view to another.
    </p>
    <p>I only see totals in the Cloudflare dashboard, and I make no decisions about individual visitors.</p>
    <p><strong>Lawful basis:</strong> legitimate interests, in understanding how the site is used so I can improve it.</p>
    <p>
      <strong>Storage and access on your device:</strong> the script reads the page address and load timings from your browser, which counts as
      accessing information on your device. PECR allows this without consent when it is only used to produce statistics for improving the site,
      you are told about it, and you can object for free (Schedule A1, paragraph 5). Cloudflare processes the data on my behalf, as my processor.
      Switching analytics off is how you object.
    </p>
    <p>
      <strong>How long it is kept:</strong> Cloudflare keeps the statistics for the limited period it sets for Web Analytics. I do not export
      or copy them.
    </p>
    <p>
      <strong>Earlier analytics:</strong> until 10 October 2026 the site used Umami, running on my own server in Finland. I deleted that data
      when I switched to Cloudflare.
    </p>

    <section aria-labelledby="turn-off" class="rounded-lg border border-border bg-card p-5">
      <h2 id="turn-off" class="pt-0!">Turning analytics off</h2>
      <p class="mt-3">Analytics does not run, and your browser never loads Cloudflare's analytics script, if any of these apply:</p>
      <ul class="mt-3">
        <li>you switch it off below, which saves your choice in this browser</li>
        <li>your browser sends a Global Privacy Control or Do Not Track signal</li>
        <li>JavaScript is turned off, or a content blocker blocks <code>static.cloudflareinsights.com</code></li>
      </ul>
      <div id="analytics-choice" class="mt-5" hidden>
        <label class="flex cursor-pointer items-start gap-3 text-foreground">
          <input id="analytics-allowed" type="checkbox" class="mt-1 size-5 shrink-0 cursor-pointer accent-foreground" />
          <span>Include my visits in the site's analytics</span>
        </label>
        <p id="analytics-status" role="status" class="mt-3 text-sm"></p>
      </div>
      <noscript>
        <p class="mt-5 text-foreground">
          JavaScript is off in your browser, so the analytics script cannot run and none of your visits are counted.
        </p>
      </noscript>
    </section>

    <h2 id="email">Emailing me</h2>
    <p>
      If you email <a href={`mailto:${EMAIL}`}>{EMAIL}</a>, I receive your email address, your name if you include it, and whatever you
      write or attach. I use it to reply to you and, if we work together, to arrange that work. Cloudflare Email Routing receives mail for
      hamzatekin.dev and forwards it to my personal inbox.
    </p>
    <p>
      <strong>Lawful basis:</strong> legitimate interests, in replying to messages. If you contact me about a job or a project, it is also to
      take steps you have asked for before we enter a contract.
    </p>
    <p>
      <strong>How long I keep it:</strong> up to two years after our last exchange, unless the law requires me to keep it longer, for example
      tax records for paid work.
    </p>

    <h2 id="cookies">Cookies and similar technologies</h2>
    <p>This site does not set any cookies. The only things it reads from or saves in your browser are:</p>
    <ul>
      <li>the page address and load timings, read by the analytics script described above</li>
      <li>
        an <code>analytics-off</code> entry in local storage, saved only if you switch analytics off. Each page checks for it so it can respect
        your choice. It stays until you switch analytics back on or clear this site's data in your browser.
      </li>
    </ul>
    <p>Cloudflare may also save a Network Error Logging policy in your browser so it can report connection failures.</p>

    <h2 id="sharing">Who else handles your data</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>
        Cloudflare, Inc. hosts the site, provides DNS, runs the analytics and routes email. It acts as my processor under its data
        processing addendum.
      </li>
      <li>My email provider stores the messages you send me.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>I do not sell personal data or share it for advertising. I would only disclose it to anyone else if the law required me to.</p>

    <h2 id="transfers">Transfers outside the UK</h2>
    <p>
      Cloudflare runs a global network and is based in the United States. It is certified under the UK Extension to the EU-US Data Privacy
      Framework, and its data processing addendum includes the ICO's International Data Transfer Addendum as a fallback. My email provider
      may store messages outside the UK, under the transfer safeguards in its own terms.
    </p>

    <h2 id="rights">Your rights</h2>
    <p>You can ask me to:</p>
    <ul>
      <li>give you a copy of the personal data I hold about you</li>
      <li>correct it if it is wrong</li>
      <li>delete it</li>
      <li>limit how I use it</li>
      <li>stop using it, as you can object at any time to anything I do on the basis of legitimate interests</li>
      <li>give it to you or another organisation in a portable format, where that applies</li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      Email <a href={`mailto:${EMAIL}`}>{EMAIL}</a>. It is free, and I will reply within one month. Analytics records hold nothing that
      shows which visits were yours, so I cannot look them up, but I will tell you what I can do.
    </p>

    <h2 id="complaints">Complaints</h2>
    <p>
      If you are unhappy with how I have handled your data, email me first. I will acknowledge your complaint within 30 days, look into it
      and tell you the outcome.
    </p>
    <p>
      You can also complain to the Information Commissioner's Office (ICO), the UK regulator, at
      <a href="https://ico.org.uk/make-a-complaint/">ico.org.uk/make-a-complaint</a> or on 0303 123 1113. If you live in the EU or EEA, you can
      complain to the data protection authority in your country.
    </p>

    <h2 id="changes">Changes to this notice</h2>
    <p>When anything here changes, I will update this page and the date at the top.</p>
  </article>

  <!-- Apart from the analytics loader (Analytics.astro), this is the site's only script: the switch has to
       write to local storage. It is progressive enhancement, so without JavaScript the <noscript> text shows. -->
  <script is:inline>
    (() => {
      const KEY = 'analytics-off';
      const LEGACY_KEY = 'umami.disabled'; // objections saved before the switch from Umami (October 2026)
      const choice = document.getElementById('analytics-choice');
      const box = document.getElementById('analytics-allowed');
      const status = document.getElementById('analytics-status');
      choice.hidden = false;

      const label = box.closest('label');
      if (navigator.globalPrivacyControl || navigator.doNotTrack === '1') {
        label.hidden = true;
        status.textContent = 'Analytics is off, because your browser sends a Global Privacy Control or Do Not Track signal.';
        return;
      }

      let storage = null;
      try {
        storage = window.localStorage;
        storage.getItem(KEY);
      } catch {
        storage = null;
      }
      if (!storage) {
        label.hidden = true;
        status.textContent =
          'Your browser blocks this site from saving data, so this switch cannot work. Turning on Global Privacy Control in your browser will stop analytics instead.';
        return;
      }

      if (storage.getItem(LEGACY_KEY)) {
        storage.setItem(KEY, '1');
        storage.removeItem(LEGACY_KEY);
      }
      box.checked = !storage.getItem(KEY);
      box.addEventListener('change', () => {
        if (box.checked) storage.removeItem(KEY);
        else storage.setItem(KEY, '1');
        status.textContent = box.checked
          ? 'Saved. Your visits will be counted again in this browser.'
          : 'Saved. Your visits will not be counted in this browser.';
      });
    })();
  </script>
</Layout>
